<section class="tight">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Architecture &amp; operating model</span>
    <h1>EZGraph vs. LangGraph</h1>
    <p class="lede">Keep LangGraph as the execution engine. Choose which application conventions your team wants supplied.</p>
    <p>EZGraph is an application layer built on LangGraph and LangChain. It brings class-based
      nodes, conversation handling, and durable session management to guided applications.
      Direct LangGraph gives your team the graph primitives and lets you choose the surrounding
      application structure.</p>
    <p>Start here for the architecture. The <a href="/ezgraph/compare/langgraph/quotegraph-case-study/">QuoteGraph case study</a>
      separately examines one insurance chatbot built both ways, with code counts, edit sites,
      and the measurement method.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="ownership">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
      <h2>The ownership choice</h2>
      <p>Both execute LangGraph graphs. EZGraph standardizes the conversation and session layer around them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>EZGraph supplies</th><th>A direct LangGraph team chooses</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Authoring</td><td>Node classes with prompts, tools, state writes, and outcomes; validated registration through <code>StateGraphExt</code>.</td><td>Node functions, state schemas, edges, and how to organize those responsibilities in application code.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conversation handling</td><td>A reusable <code>ConversationNode</code> model/tool loop and response contracts.</td><td>How to compose model calls, tool handling, validation, and response policy, including available LangChain helpers.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Session lifecycle</td><td><code>GraphEngine</code> coordinates restore, invocation, progress persistence, and final saves through a session manager.</td><td>The invocation boundary and how graph execution connects to application sessions and storage.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Execution</td><td>A class-based authoring layer that compiles to a LangGraph <code>StateGraph</code>.</td><td>Direct use of graph topology, channels, reducers, and execution controls.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-32">LangGraph's <a href="https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langgraph/graph-api">Graph API</a>
      defines the underlying execution model. Adding EZGraph changes the application contract;
      it does not replace that scheduler with PicoFlow's Flow/Step runtime.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="state-and-sessions">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Operating model</span>
      <h2>State, memory, and sessions</h2>
      <p>A shared session convention gives each conversation an application-readable operating record.</p>
    </div>
    <p>EZGraph keeps business data in graph-owned state, with node-owned channels and named
      histories. Node classes group behavior; mutable instance fields are not a place to keep
      one customer's conversation state, because graph and node objects serve multiple sessions.</p>
    <p>The session layer restores the saved conversation and records progress and diagnostics.
      This provides a common boundary for continuing a conversation, inspecting a failed turn,
      and applying application-specific restore policy. See the
      <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/">developer guide</a> for state ownership and node contracts.</p>
    <div class="callout tip">
      <span class="label">Session records and checkpoints serve different boundaries</span>
      <p>EZGraph's normal session persistence is an application-level record. It is not a promise
        of LangGraph checkpoint history or arbitrary mid-node resume. Direct LangGraph offers
        <a href="https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langgraph/persistence">checkpointers and stores</a>;
        a team can use them alongside its own domain records. Choose the recovery and inspection
        boundaries your application needs.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="conversation-contracts">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Application contracts</span>
      <h2>One convention across conversation stages</h2>
      <p>A node brings its prompt, allowed tools, validation, and transition decision together.</p>
    </div>
    <p>In a guided conversation, collecting information is only part of the work. A stage must
      decide whether input is valid, whether to ask again, whether to return a direct response,
      and when to advance. EZGraph supplies common node and tool contracts so those decisions
      can be expressed consistently across stages.</p>
    <p>The business policy remains application code: eligibility checks, pricing, authorization,
      and external effects still need explicit handlers. Direct LangGraph can implement the
      same behavior through node functions and shared helpers. The architectural choice is
      whether EZGraph's existing conventions fit the product and the team's preferred authoring model.</p>
    <p>Follow the <a href="/ezgraph/tutorial/">small tutorial</a> to see the contracts in use,
      or the <a href="/ezgraph/quote-graph/">QuoteGraph walkthrough</a> for a complete staged conversation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="choosing">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Decision guide</span>
      <h2>Which layer should your team own?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Choose EZGraph when…</h3>
        <p>You want guided, multi-turn applications on LangGraph with reusable node, tool,
          state, and session conventions. The value is a common application baseline that
          your team can apply across conversations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Choose direct LangGraph when…</h3>
        <p>You want to design the graph and surrounding application contracts directly, or
          already have a platform that supplies those conventions. Check the fit carefully
          when custom execution and checkpoint behavior are central requirements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-32">The <a href="/ezgraph/#limits">EZGraph limits</a> describe the abstraction's
      boundaries. The <a href="/ezgraph/compare/langgraph/quotegraph-case-study/">case study</a>
      provides evidence for one implementation; its code reduction is not a universal
      performance, quality, or productivity result.</p>
